Article 12: Where a party, in accordance with the Second Paragraph of Article 13 of the Trademark Law, claims that the trademark in dispute constitutes a reproduction, imitation, or translation of its unregistered well-known trademark and therefore shall not be registered or shall be invalidated, the People's Court shall comprehensively consider the following factors and the mutual influence between the factors to determine whether confusion is likely to be caused:
(1) the degree of similarity of the trademark marks;
(2) the degree of similarity of the goods;
(3) the distinctiveness and degree of fame of the trademark for which protection is requested;
(4) the degree of attention of the relevant public;
(5) other relevant factors.
The subjective intent of the trademark applicant and evidence of actual confusion may serve as reference factors for determining the likelihood of confusion.

Article 13: Where a party, in accordance with the Third Paragraph of Article 13 of the Trademark Law, claims that the trademark in dispute constitutes a reproduction, imitation, or translation of its registered well-known trademark and therefore shall not be registered or shall be invalidated, the People's Court shall comprehensively consider the following factors to determine whether the use of the trademark in dispute is sufficient to make the relevant public believe that it has a considerable degree of connection with the well-known trademark, thereby misleading the public and causing the interests of the registrant of the well-known trademark to be possibly damaged:
(1) the distinctiveness and degree of fame of the cited trademark;
(2) whether the trademark mark is sufficiently similar;
(3) the situation of the designated goods;
(4) the degree of overlap and degree of attention of the relevant public;
(5) the situation in which marks similar to the cited trademark are lawfully used by other market entities, or other relevant factors.

Article 16: The following circumstances may be determined as the 'other relationships' prescribed in the Second Paragraph of Article 15 of the Trademark Law:
(1) there is a kinship between the trademark applicant and the prior user;
(2) there is an employment relationship between the trademark applicant and the prior user;
(3) the business addresses of the trademark applicant and the prior user are adjacent;
(4) the trademark applicant and the prior user have previously negotiated to establish an agency or representative relationship, but have not formed an agency or representative relationship;
(5) the trademark applicant and the prior user have previously negotiated to reach a contract or business transaction relationship, but have not reached a contract or business transaction relationship.

Article 27: Where a party claims that the following circumstances of the National Intellectual Property Administration fall under the 'violation of statutory procedures' prescribed in Item (3) of Article 70 of the Administrative Procedure Law, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) omitting the review reasons raised by the party, which has an actual impact on the party's rights;
(2) failing to notify the member of the collegiate panel during the review procedure, and upon examination, there is indeed a ground for withdrawal that has not been withdrawn;
(3) failing to notify the eligible party to participate in the review, and that party explicitly raises an objection;
(4) other circumstances that violate statutory procedures.
